In a quirks-mode document, Ladybird now records which margins of an
element the user agent’s style sheet sets, and a quirks-mode body
or table cell leaves out the quirky ones among them (block-start
margins of the elements with default margins, and block-end margins
of p and the preformatted elements) where they collapse into its own
block start or block end. Blink does the same: Its html.css gives
those margins in the quirky __qem unit, which its ComputedStyle::
HasMarginBlockStartQuirk() and HasMarginBlockEndQuirk() read; and
BlockLayoutAlgorithm sets is_quirky_container_start on the margin
strut of a BlockNode::IsQuirkyContainer() (a quirks-mode body or table
cell), MarginStrut::Append() drops quirky margins while it’s set, and
the container sums its block-end margins with QuirkyContainerSum(),
which leaves them out. WebKit’s html.css uses the same unit, and
RenderBlockFlow::collapseMarginsWithChildInfo() ignores a quirky
margin in a MarginInfo whose quirkContainer() is a table cell or the
body. Gecko follows the spec’s text instead: Its quirk.css zeroes
the margins of the children of body, td and th with :-moz-first-node,
:-moz-last-node and :-moz-only-whitespace. On 35 static cases and
8 dynamic changes, Ladybird now gives exactly the results of Chrome
158, which WebKit 26.5 shares on all but one. Firefox 151 agrees on
the dynamic changes, and its quirk gives other results on 13 of the
static cases: nested paragraphs, a span or script before a paragraph,
floated and inline-block paragraphs, pre, empty paragraphs, and a
“display: table-cell” box.
